{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqh2eifaveh","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"CORRECTION: orchestrator merge does NOT stash; FU-2 code corruption is an external git stash on the shared checkout","summary":"OVERRIDE: this supersedes the earlier claim that the attempt-merge automation git-stashes. Verified from code: mergeBranch + mergeFeature (lib/git.js:176 and :218) run `git merge --no-ff` in the workspace and ABORT to a clean tree on ANY failure (dirty tree OR conflict) — they NEVER stash, and a merge on a dirty tree simply fails+aborts without touching uncommitted work. A grep across .js/.sh/.json/.md found ZERO `git stash` anywhere in the codebase (only the orch-gate-bash hook that BLOCKS stash for subagents). Therefore the `git stash` + `<<<<<<< Updated upstream / Stashed changes` corruption that hit interactive edits came from an EXTERNAL actor sharing the single working tree — most likely a concurrent INTERACTIVE Claude session (main sessions are not claim-gated like subagents) running `git stash` as part of its own flow; evidence: a live `stash@{0}: WIP on main`. CONSEQUENCE: 'merge attempts in a dedicated worktree' (Option 1) and 'don't stash during merge' (Option 2) target a mechanism that does not exist and would NOT fix the corruption. The correct fix is INTERACTIVE-SESSION ISOLATION: give each interactive session its own git worktree (the orchestrator already isolates ATTEMPTS via createWorktree; EnterWorktree exists for interactive relocation) so one session's working-tree git ops cannot corrupt another's. Caveat: session isolation fixes the CODE-FILE corruption only; the distinct .graph desync (version-controlled .graph written live by the daemon while attempts merge into the same checkout) is a separate, deeper issue. Deadlock half of FU-2 remains fixed and live (commit 751d58e, daemon restarted).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T19:58:32.134Z","vec":[-0.03930455446243286,-0.01131608709692955,0.020143600180745125,-0.01068953238427639,0